Subject: lottie: ease each dimension of a keyframe with its own curve

After Effects lets each dimension of a multi-dimensional property (scale, rectangle size,
anchor...) have its own speed curve, and Lottie exports it as arrays in the keyframe's easing
(`"i": {"x": [0.7, 0.8], "y": [1, 0.7]}`). ThorVG read only the first value, so every dimension
followed the first one's curve: a squash animated differently in x and y came out with the wrong
proportions mid-way (Lordicon: 2 062 of 17 860 public icons have such keyframes).

The parser keeps the second dimension's tangents and, when they differ, gives the frame a second
interpolator; scalar frames of points ease x with the first and y (and z) with the second.
Spatial (position) frames keep one curve, as in After Effects.

Origin: Lordicon.
Base: thorvg/thorvg main 72a66415 (1.2.0). License: MIT, like ThorVG.
